e a picture with the right number of pieces but the wrong shape","COUNT-SIDES":"Miscounted sides or corners","DECADE":"Lost the count at a new ten (29, 20 or 29, 40)","DIV-AS-SUB":"Subtracted once instead of dividing","DROP-ADDEND":"Left out one of the numbers being added","DROP-ZERO":"Dropped a zero when multiplying by tens","ENDS-NOT-ALIGNED":"Compared lengths without lining up the ends","EQ-ALL-NUMBERS":"Added every number on both sides","EQ-ANSWER-NEXT":"Read = as 'the answer comes next' (judged 5 = 2 + 3 false)","EXACT-NOT-ESTIMATE":"Worked the exact answer instead of estimating","EXPANDED-AS-DIGITS":"Wrote expanded form as the digits only (3 + 4 + 5)","EXTRA-ZERO":"Added an extra zero","FACT-NEIGHBOR":"Gave a neighboring fact (a nearby answer from the same table)","FLAT-SOLID":"Mixed up a flat shape and a solid (square and cube)","FR-ADD-SAME":"Added the same number to top and bottom to make an 'equal' fraction","FR-BIGGER-DENOM":"Thought a bigger bottom number means a bigger fraction","FR-COUNT-MARKS":"Counted the marks on the number line, not the spaces","FR-INVERT":"Wrote the fraction upside down","FR-PARTS-NOT-WHOLE":"Named a fraction as pieces shaded over pieces unshaded","FR-WHOLE-NUMBER":"Treated the top and bottom as separate whole numbers","GAPS-OVERLAPS":"Left gaps or overlaps between units when measuring","GIVE-PRODUCT":"Gave the product instead of the missing factor","GIVE-WHOLE":"Gave the whole (or the given number) instead of the missing part","GRAPH-SCALE":"Read a scaled graph as one per picture or one per line","HALF-NEXT-HOUR":"At half past, named the next hour","HOUR-ONLY":"Counted only the hours","HUNDRED-WRITE":"Wrote numbers past 100 as spoken (1004 for 104)","KEYWORD":"Chose the operation from a key word, not the story","MAKE10-LEFTOVER":"Made ten but lost the leftover","MIN-AS-NUMBER":"Read the minute hand as the number it points to (at 3 means 3 minutes)","MORE-LESS":"Mixed up 'more' and 'fewer'","MUL-ADD":"Added instead of multiplying (6 x 4 = 10)","NAME-SOUND":"Confused numbers that sound alike (13 and 30, 14 and 40)","NEAR-DOUBLE":"Used the double but did not adjust (6+7 as 12)","NO-REGROUP-ADD":"Forgot to carry (47 + 28 = 65)","ODD-EVEN-FIRST-DIGIT":"Judged odd or even by the first digit","ODD-EVEN-SWAP":"Mixed up odd and even","OFF1":"Off by one: counted the starting number or stopped one early","ONE-STEP-ONLY":"Did only the first step of a two-step problem","OP-SWAP":"Used the wrong operation (added instead of subtracting, and so on)","ORIENT":"Did not know a shape when it is turned","PART-WHOLE":"Gave a part that does not match the whole","PATTERN-ADD-NOT-RULE":"Continued by the last difference, not the rule","POS-NEAR":"Chose a nearby position (beside for in front of)","POS-OPPOSITE":"Chose the opposite position (above for below)","PV-COUNT-ALL":"Counted all the blocks as ones","PV-SWAP":"Swapped tens and ones (3 tens 5 ones as 53)","PV-ZERO":"Dropped or added a zero that holds a place (305 as 35)","READ-ONE-BAR":"Read one bar or row instead of comparing two","REGROUP-ZERO":"Could not regroup across a zero (400 - 123)","RESTART-AT-1":"Started counting from 1 instead of the given number","REV":"Wrote the digits in reverse (12 for 21)","ROUND-DOWN-ALWAYS":"Always rounded down","ROUND-WRONG-PLACE":"Rounded to the wrong place","ROWS-PLUS-COLS":"Added rows and columns instead of multiplying","RULER-FROM-1":"Measured from 1 on the ruler instead of 0","SIGN-DIRECTION":"Pointed the < or > sign the wrong way","SIZE-NOT-COUNT":"Chose the group that looks bigger, not the one with more","SKIP-BY-1":"Counted on by one instead of skip counting","SORT-WRONG-RULE":"Sorted by a different rule than the one asked","SQUARE-NOT-RECT":"Thought a square is not a rectangle (or rhombus)","SUB-SMALLER-FROM-LARGER":"Took the smaller digit from the larger in each column (52 - 17 = 45)","SUB-WRONG-PLACE":"Took tens away from the ones place","TEEN-AS-1":"Read a teen as 1 and some ones instead of ten and some ones","TIME-BASE10":"Treated time as base ten (1:50 + 20 minutes = 1:70)","TIME-HANDS":"Mixed up the hour hand and the minute hand","TWO-SIDES-ONLY":"Added only two sides for perimeter","UNEQUAL-PARTS":"Accepted unequal parts as halves or quarters","UNIT-MIX":"Mixed two units in one answer","ZERO-ONE":"Mixed up times 0 and times 1","BLEND-DROP":"Dropped a sound from a blend (sop for stop)","CASE-MIX":"Matched the wrong capital and small letters","CATEGORY-TOO-BROAD":"Named a group that is too broad","DETAIL-TRUE-NOT-ANSWER":"A true statement from the text that does not answer the question","DETAIL-WRONG":"Chose a detail that is not in the text","DIGRAPH":"Read a digraph as two separate letters (s-h)","ENDING":"Ignored or changed the ending","FIRST-LETTER":"Guessed from the first letter (cot for cat)","FIRST-PART-ONLY":"Read only the first part of a two-part word","FIRST-SOUND-ONLY":"Used only the first sound","LAST-SOUND":"Gave the last sound instead of the first","LETTER-NAME":"Gave the letter name instead of its sound (or picked a word starting with the letter name, like 'el' for l)","LONG-FOR-SHORT":"Gave the long vowel (letter name) for a short vowel","ONE-WORD-MATCH":"Matched one word in the sentence, not the whole sentence","PRINT-DIRECTION":"Not sure where to start or which way print goes","PRIOR-KNOWLEDGE":"Answered from what he knows, not from the text","REAL-WORD-SUB":"On a made-up word, chose a real word that looks like it: reading by memory, not decoding","REVERSAL":"Reversed a look-alike letter or word (b/d, p/q, was/saw)","RHYME-MEANING":"Chose a word that goes with it in meaning","RHYME-ONSET":"Chose a word with the same first sound, not the same ending","SEG-OFF1":"Gave one sound too many or too few","SEG-ONSET-RIME":"Split only into first sound and the rest (c-at)","SEQ":"Put events in the wrong order","SETTING-CHARACTER":"Mixed up a character and the setting","SILENT-E":"Ignored the silent e (cap for cape)","SWAP-WRONG-POSITION":"Changed the sound in the wrong place","SYLL-LETTERS":"Counted letters or vowels instead of beats","SYLL-OFF1":"One beat off","VISUAL-SIM":"Chose a word that looks similar","VOICED-PAIR":"Confused a sound with its voiced partner (p/b, t/d, f/v)","VOWEL":"Wrong vowel sound","VTEAM":"Misread a vowel team","WORD-ASSOC":"Chose a word that goes with the target instead of one that means it","WORD-LETTER":"Mixed up a word and a letter","R-CTRL":"Mixed up the r-controlled vowel (ar / or / ir / ur / er)","Y-SOUND":"Read y at the end of the word wrong (or skipped it)","SYLL-SPLIT":"Split the word into syllables in the wrong place","HARD-SOFT-CG":"Mixed up hard and soft c or g","SILENT-LETTER":"Said a letter that is silent (kn, wr, gh)","PREFIX-IGNORED":"Ignored the prefix and read only the base word","PREFIX-OPPOSITE":"Gave the prefix the wrong meaning","SUFFIX-IGNORED":"Ignored the suffix or gave it the wrong meaning","LESSON-TOO-NARROW":"Picked one detail instead of the lesson of the whole story","TOO-NARROW":"Picked one part instead of the main topic","TOO-BROAD":"Picked something too big to be the main topic","FEATURE-PURPOSE":"Did not know which text feature (title, heading, caption) holds the information","ALPHA-ORDER":"ABC order mistake (checked only the first letter, or went the wrong way)","WRONG-SENSE":"Picked a meaning of the word that does not fit the sentence","LITERAL-FIG":"Took a figure of speech word for word","TRAIT-FEELING":"Picked a passing feeling instead of a character trait","CONTEXT-IGNORED":"Did not use the clues in the sentence","QUESTION-WORD":"Answered a different question word (when / where / who)","ABS":"Treated absolute value as 'the opposite'","ANALOGY-RELATION":"Wrong relationship in an analogy","ANGLE-PAIR-TYPE":"Mixed up angle pairs (supplementary, complementary, vertical)","ANGLE-SIZE-LENGTH":"Judged an angle by the length of its sides","ANGLE-SUM-360":"Used 360 for a triangle's angle sum","AREA-NOT-VOL":"Found an area when volume was asked (or the reverse)","AREA-SCALE-LINEAR":"Scaled area by the length factor, not its square","BOX-QUARTILE":"Misread a box plot's quartiles","CAUSE-EFFECT-REVERSED":"Reversed cause and effect","CIRC-AREA-CIRCUM":"Mixed up area and circumference","CIRC-RD":"Used the diameter for the radius, or the reverse","CLAIM-REASON-SWAP":"Mixed up the claim and a reason","COMBINE-UNLIKE":"Combined unlike terms (3x + 2 = 5x)","COMPARE-ONE-SIDE":"Described only one side of the comparison","CONE-NO-THIRD":"Forgot the one third for a cone","COORD-COUNT-LINES":"Counted grid lines from 1, not 0","COORD-SWAP":"Swapped x and y","CUBE-THIRD":"Divided by 3 instead of the cube root","DEC-ALIGN":"Lined up the last digits instead of the decimal points","DEC-DIGITS-AS-FR":"Wrote a decimal's digits as the fraction (0.3 as 1/3)","DEC-LONGER-BIGGER":"Thought a longer decimal is bigger (0.25 > 0.5)","DEC-POINT-PLACE":"Put the decimal point in the wrong place","DEC-SHORTER-BIGGER":"Thought a shorter decimal is bigger (0.4 > 0.45)","DEC-TENTH-HUNDREDTH":"Mixed up tenths and hundredths (0.3 as 3/100)","DIST-PARTIAL":"Multiplied only the first term inside the parentheses","DIV-REM":"Mishandled the remainder (ignored it, or rounded the wrong way for the story)","EQ-INVERSE":"Did the same operation instead of the inverse (x + 5 = 12 gives 17)","EQ-ORDER-STEPS":"Undid steps in the wrong order","EVIDENCE-IRRELEVANT":"Accepted irrelevant evidence","EVIDENCE-TRUE-NOT-SUPPORT":"Chose true evidence that does not support the answer","EXP-ADD-WRONG":"Added exponents when it should multiply them, or the reverse","EXP-MULT":"Multiplied base by exponent (10^3 as 30)","EXPR-REVERSED":"Wrote the expression in reverse order (3 less than x as 3 - x)","FACTOR-MULTIPLE":"Mixed up factors and multiples","FR-ADD-BOTH":"Added tops and bottoms (1/4 + 2/4 = 3/8)","FR-CROSS-MULT":"Cross-multiplied when multiplying fractions","FR-INVERT-WRONG":"Flipped the first fraction instead of the second","FR-MULT-BOTH":"Multiplied top and bottom by the whole number","FR-NO-COMMON":"Did not find a common denominator","FR-NO-INVERT":"Divided fractions without flipping the second","FUNC-REPEAT-OUTPUT":"Thought a repeated output breaks a function","FUNC-VERTICAL":"Did not recognize one input with two outputs","GCF-LCM-SWAP":"Gave the GCF for the LCM, or the reverse","GRAPHIC-MISREAD":"Misread a chart, diagram or timeline","INFER-LITERAL":"Gave a literal fact when an inference was asked","INFER-TOO-FAR":"Inferred more than the clues support","INTERCEPT-SWAP":"Swapped slope and starting value","LONGDIV-ZERO":"Left out a zero in the quotient","MEAN-MEDIAN":"Mixed up mean and median","MEAN-SUM":"Gave the total instead of the fair share","MEDIAN-UNSORTED":"Took the middle value without ordering the numbers","MIX-CONVERT":"Converted a mixed number wrongly (2 3/4 as 5/4 or 6/4)","MULT-NO-SHIFT":"Did not shift the second partial product","MULT-PARTIAL":"Left out a partial product","NAME-IN-TEXT":"Thought a named character must be the narrator","NARRATOR-POV":"Mixed up who is telling","NEG-ORDER":"Ordered negatives by their digits (-8 > -3)","NEG-SIGN":"Sign error with negative numbers","NEG-SUB-AS-ADD":"Treated subtracting a negative as subtracting","ONE-IDEA-ONLY":"Found only one of the main ideas","ONE-IS-PRIME":"Called 1 prime, or 2 composite","ONE-SOURCE-ONLY":"Used only one of the two texts","ORD-OPS":"Worked left to right ignoring order of operations","PARALLEL-PERP":"Mixed up parallel and perpendicular","PAREN-IGNORED":"Ignored the parentheses","PART-PURPOSE":"Did not see why a part is there","PCT-AS-NUM":"Treated a percent as a number to add or subtract (20% of 50 = 30)","PCT-CHANGE-BASE":"Used the new amount as the base of percent change","PCT-WHOLE-PART":"Mixed up the part and the whole","PI-RATIONAL":"Thought pi (or 22/7) is exact and rational","PROB-ADD-NOT-MULT":"Added probabilities that should be multiplied","PROB-COUNT":"Wrong number of possible outcomes","PRODUCT-AS-LCM":"Gave the product as the LCM","PROTRACTOR-SCALE":"Read the wrong scale on the protractor","PURPOSE-TOPIC":"Gave the topic instead of the purpose","PYTH-ADD":"Added the legs instead of squaring (a + b = c)","PYTH-LEG-HYP":"Treated a leg as the longest side","RATE-INVERT":"Divided the wrong way for a unit rate","RATIO-ADD":"Added a fixed amount instead of multiplying (additive, not multiplicative)","RATIO-ORDER":"Wrote the ratio in the wrong order","REASON-NOT-SUPPORT":"Chose a reason that does not support the point","REFLECT-ROTATE":"Mixed up a reflection and a rotation","REPEAT-AS-END":"Treated a repeating decimal as ending","ROOT-ALWAYS-IRR":"Thought every square root is irrational","ROOT-NEAREST-WRONG":"Placed a root between the wrong whole numbers","ROOT-WRONG":"Wrong meaning for the root","SA-MISSING-FACE":"Left out a face","SAMPLE-BIAS":"Chose a biased sample","SCALE-INVERT":"Used the scale the wrong way","SIGN-RULE-ADD":"Used the addition sign rule for multiplication","SIMILE-METAPHOR":"Mixed up simile and metaphor","SLANT-AS-HEIGHT":"Used a slanted side as the height","SLOPE-INV":"Used run over rise","SOURCE-RELIABILITY":"Trusted an unreliable source","SQRT-HALF":"Halved instead of taking the square root","STEP-ORDER":"Out-of-order step in a procedure","STRUCTURE-MIX":"Mixed up two text structures","STRUCTURE-TOPIC":"Named the topic instead of the structure","SUMMARY-DETAIL":"Summary full of small details","SUMMARY-OPINION":"Summary includes opinion","SYM-DIAGONAL":"Called a rectangle's diagonal a line of symmetry","SYN-ANT-SWAP":"Mixed up synonym and antonym","SYS-ONE-VAR":"Solved for one variable and stopped","TABLE-ROW-COL":"Read the wrong row or column total","THEME-DETAIL":"Gave a plot detail instead of the theme","THEME-TOPIC":"Gave the topic instead of the theme","TONE-MISREAD":"Misread the tone","TREND-CAUSE":"Read a trend as proof of a cause","TREND-THROUGH-POINTS":"Chose a line through two points rather than the best fit","TRI-HALF":"Forgot the half in a triangle's area","UNIT-CONV-DIR":"Divided instead of multiplying when converting units, or the reverse","UNIT-CONV-FACTOR":"Used the wrong conversion number (100 cm in a foot)","VOL-ADD":"Added length, width and height"},"passages":{"PF.R.L01.S.A":{"id":"PF.R.L01.S.A","title":"Dovid's New Kippah","text":"Dovid got a new blue kippah. He wore it to shul on Shabbos. On the way, the wind blew it off his head! Dovid ran after it. His brother Avi caught it by the gate. \"Thank you, Avi,\" said Dovid. In shul, Dovid held his kippah tight.","listen":true,"kind":"story","status":"Approved"},"PF.R.L01.S.B":{"id":"PF.R.L01.S.B","title":"Yossi's Lost Mitten","text":"Yossi had two red mittens. After cheder, he could find only one. He looked in his bag. He looked under his chair. Then his rebbe held up a red mitten. \"Is this yours, Yossi?\" Yossi smiled. Now he had two mittens again, and his hands were warm.","listen":true,"kind":"story","status":"Approved"},"PF.R.L01.I.A":{"id":"PF.R.L01.I.A","title":"Bees","text":"Bees fly from flower to flower. They drink a sweet juice called nectar. Bees take the nectar home to the hive. In the hive, the nectar turns into honey. Hashem made bees so busy! On Rosh Hashanah we dip apples in honey.","listen":true,"kind":"info","status":"Approved"},"PF.R.L01.I.B":{"id":"PF.R.L01.I.B","title":"Apples","text":"Apples grow on trees. In spring, the tree has white flowers. The flowers turn into small green apples. All summer the apples grow big. In the fall, they are ready to pick. Before we eat an apple, we make a bracha.","listen":true,"kind":"info","status":"Approved"},"PF.R.L02.S.A":{"id":"PF.R.L02.S.A","title":"The Big Hill","text":"Yossi has a red sled. It is cold and the hill is white with snow. Yossi and his dad go up the hill. \"Hold on tight!\" says Dad. Yossi sits on the sled. Swish! Down he goes, fast, fast, fast. At the end he falls in the snow. He gets up and grins. \"Can we go again?\"","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Approved"},"PF.R.L02.S.B":{"id":"PF.R.L02.S.B","title":"The Lost Siddur","text":"Chaim had a new siddur with a green cover. On Sunday he could not find it. He looked on his desk. He looked in his bed. Then his little brother Eli came in. Eli had the siddur! \"I wanted to look at the pictures,\" said Eli. Chaim sat down with Eli, and they looked at it together.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Approved"},"PF.R.L02.I.A":{"id":"PF.R.L02.I.A","title":"Frogs","text":"A frog starts as a tiny egg in a pond. The egg hatches into a tadpole. A tadpole has a tail and swims like a fish. Then it grows back legs. Next it grows front legs. Last, the tail gets small, and it is a frog! A frog can hop on land and swim in water.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Approved"},"PF.R.L02.I.B":{"id":"PF.R.L02.I.B","title":"Snow","text":"Snow falls when it is very cold. Each snowflake is made of tiny bits of ice. No two snowflakes look the same. Snow can be soft and light, or wet and heavy. When the sun comes out and it gets warm, snow melts into water. Then it goes into the ground.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Approved"},"PF.R.L03.S.A":{"id":"PF.R.L03.S.A","title":"Yaakov Builds the Sukkah","text":"The day after Yom Kippur, Abba took out the sukkah boards. Yaakov wanted to help, but the boards were too heavy for him. \"What can I do?\" he asked. Abba smiled. \"You can be in charge of the bolts.\" Yaakov held the box of bolts and handed Abba one each time he called. When the walls were up, Yaakov and his brothers put the schach on top. That night the family ate in the sukkah. Abba raised his cup. \"This sukkah stands because of every helper, big and small,\" he said. Yaakov sat up tall. Even a small job, he thought, can be a big help.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft"},"PF.R.L03.S.B":{"id":"PF.R.L03.S.B","title":"The Slow Walk to Shul","text":"Every Shabbos, Shmuel walked to shul with his Zaidy. Zaidy walked slowly, with a cane. Shmuel's friends ran ahead and called, \"Come on, Shmuel!\" Shmuel wanted to run with them. But he looked at Zaidy and stayed by his side. They talked about the parsha as they walked. When they got to shul, Zaidy put his hand on Shmuel's shoulder. \"Thank you for walking with me,\" he said. \"It is the best part of my Shabbos.\" Shmuel smiled. Running would have been fun, but this felt even better.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft"},"PF.R.L03.I.A":{"id":"PF.R.L03.I.A","title":"Beavers, the Builders","text":"Teeth for the Job\nHashem made the beaver a builder. Its front teeth are orange and very strong, and they never stop growing. A beaver can chew through a small tree in one night.\nBuilding a Dam\nBeavers pile up sticks, mud and stones across a stream. The water backs up and makes a pond. In the pond, the beaver builds its home, called a lodge.\nPicture caption: A beaver lodge has an underwater door.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft"},"PF.R.L03.I.B":{"id":"PF.R.L03.I.B","title":"Maple Syrup","text":"From the Tree\nMaple trees store sweet water called sap. In late winter, when the days are warm and the nights are cold, the sap starts to flow. Farmers make a small hole in the tree and let the sap drip into a bucket.\nBoiling It Down\nIt takes about 40 buckets of sap to make one bucket of syrup. The sap is boiled for hours until most of the water is gone. What is left is thick, sweet maple syrup.\nPicture caption: A bucket catches the sap.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft"},"PF.R.L04.S.A":{"id":"PF.R.L04.S.A","title":"The Brown Wallet","text":"On the way home from cheder, Ari spotted a brown wallet lying near the bus stop. He picked it up and peeked inside. There were three twenty-dollar bills and a card with a name: Mr. Levin, 14 Park Road. Ari's heart thumped. Sixty dollars! He thought about the new baseball glove in the store window. Then he thought about Mr. Levin, searching his pockets and feeling sick with worry. Ari walked the four blocks to Park Road. An older man answered the door, looking frazzled, his hair sticking up and his glasses crooked. \"I've been looking everywhere!\" he said when he saw the wallet. \"That money was for my grandson's bar mitzvah gift.\" Mr. Levin tried to give Ari a reward, but Ari shook his head. \"Hashavas aveidah is a mitzvah,\" he said. \"I don't need to be paid for it.\" Walking home, Ari still did not have a new glove. But he felt lighter than he had all day.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft"},"PF.R.L04.S.B":{"id":"PF.R.L04.S.B","title":"The Craft-Stick Bridge","text":"Nosson had worked for two weeks on his model bridge for the class project. It was made of craft sticks and glue, and he was sure it could hold a pile of seforim. On project day, Rebbi placed the first sefer on top. Crack! The bridge sagged and snapped in the middle. Nosson's face turned red. He wanted to hide the pieces in his desk. Instead, he looked closely at the break. The sticks had all been glued end to end in one long line, with nothing holding them from the sides. That night Nosson rebuilt the bridge. This time he glued the sticks into triangles, the way he had seen on real bridges. The next day Rebbi stacked one sefer, then two, then five. The bridge held. \"What changed?\" Rebbi asked. Nosson grinned. \"The first bridge taught me what not to do.\"","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft"},"PF.R.L04.I.A":{"id":"PF.R.L04.I.A","title":"The Sofer","text":"A sofer is a scribe who writes a Sefer Torah by hand. It is slow, careful work. A complete Sefer Torah has 304,805 letters, and every letter must be written correctly. If even one letter is missing, or touches another letter where it should not, the Sefer Torah may not be used until it is fixed. The sofer writes on parchment, called klaf, made from the skin of a kosher animal. He uses special black ink and a quill made from a feather. Before he writes Hashem's name, the sofer says that he is writing it for the holiness of the Name. Writing a Sefer Torah usually takes about a year. When it is finished, the community celebrates with a hachnasas Sefer Torah: music, dancing, and a walk to the shul with the new Sefer Torah under a chuppah.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft"},"PF.R.L04.I.B":{"id":"PF.R.L04.I.B","title":"Lighthouses","text":"Long ago, sailing at night near a rocky coast was dangerous. Ships could not see the rocks until it was too late. So people built lighthouses: tall towers with a bright light at the top. A lighthouse keeper lived beside the tower. Every evening he climbed the winding stairs, lit the lamp and kept it burning until sunrise. In fog, when the light was hard to see, he also sounded a loud horn. Each lighthouse flashed its light in its own pattern, such as two quick flashes and then darkness. Sailors could look at a chart, match the pattern, and know exactly which lighthouse they were seeing, and so where they were. Today most lighthouses run by themselves, with electric lights and timers. Many old keepers' houses have become museums, where visitors can climb the stairs and look out over the sea.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft"},"PF.R.L05.S.A":{"id":"PF.R.L05.S.A","title":"A Snow Day for Chesed","text":"The snow started on Tuesday night, and by morning it was up to my knees. Yeshiva was closed. My little brother Meir cheered and grabbed his sled, and I was right behind him, pulling on my boots. Then I looked across the street. Mr. Feld, our neighbor, was standing on his porch with a shovel. Mr. Feld is eighty-one years old, and last winter he slipped on the ice and broke his wrist. He took one slow step down, then stopped and leaned on the railing.\n\"Meir,\" I said, \"the sledding hill can wait.\"\nMeir groaned, but he came. I shoveled the long front path while Meir swept the snow off the steps with a broom. Our fingers were freezing, and it took almost an hour. Mr. Feld watched from his window and waved every few minutes. When we finished, he opened the door and handed us each a mug of hot cocoa. \"You boys saved my morning,\" he said. \"Now I can get to shul for Mincha without worrying.\"\nBy the time we reached the sledding hill, the snow was packed down and slippery, perfect for racing. Meir beat me twice. I did not mind at all. That afternoon I learned that a snow day is not only for having fun. It can also be a day for chesed, and the fun will still be waiting afterward.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":5,"words":228},"PF.R.L05.S.B":{"id":"PF.R.L05.S.B","title":"The Siddur on the Bus","text":"On the first day of camp, I left my new siddur on the bus. It was a gift from my Zaidy, with my name stamped in gold on the cover. By the time I noticed it was gone, the bus had already driven away. I felt sick.\nMy bunkmate Dov saw my face. \"What's wrong?\" he asked. When I told him, he did not laugh or shrug. He ran with me to the camp office. The head counselor, Rabbi Gold, called the bus company. \"The driver will check,\" he said, \"but it might take until tomorrow.\"\nThat night at Maariv, I davened from a camp siddur. It was fine, but it was not mine. I kept thinking about the gold letters on my cover. Dov leaned over and whispered, \"Tomorrow it'll come back. You'll see.\"\nThe next morning, Rabbi Gold walked into the dining room holding something up high. It was my siddur! \"Shimmy, I believe this is yours,\" he said with a smile. The whole bunk clapped. I thanked Rabbi Gold, and then I thanked Dov, because he was the first one who helped me when I was scared.\nSince then, I always check my seat before I get off a bus. And whenever I see a new boy at camp who looks worried, I remember Dov. I walk over, and I ask him, \"What's wrong?\"","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":5,"words":227},"PF.R.L05.I.A":{"id":"PF.R.L05.I.A","title":"How Honey Gets to Your Table","text":"Every Rosh Hashanah, we dip apples in honey and ask for a sweet new year. But how does honey get into the jar? It is a long trip, and the bees do most of the work.\nFirst, a worker bee flies out of the hive to find flowers. With its long tongue, it sips a sweet liquid called nectar and stores it in a special honey stomach. One bee may visit more than 50 flowers on a single trip.\nNext, the bee flies home and passes the nectar to a younger bee inside the hive. That bee chews the nectar for about half an hour, which begins to change it into honey.\nThen the bees spread the nectar into the small wax cells of the honeycomb. Nectar is mostly water, so the bees fan it with their wings. The moving air dries the nectar until it becomes thick and sticky.\nFinally, when the honey is ready, the bees seal each cell with a cap of wax to keep it clean. Later, a beekeeper takes the frames out of the hive, cuts off the wax caps and spins the frames in a machine. The honey flows out, is strained, and is poured into jars. The next time you taste honey, think of the thousands of trips that went into every spoonful.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":5,"words":220,"onScreen":"Box beside the article — \"One jar of honey (1 pound)\": flowers visited: about 2 million; miles flown: about 55,000.","extras":[{"k":"box","title":"One jar of honey (1 pound)","lines":["flowers visited: about 2 million","miles flown: about 55,000"]}]},"PF.R.L05.I.B":{"id":"PF.R.L05.I.B","title":"Why Leaves Change Color","text":"In the fall, the trees in Baltimore turn yellow, orange and red. Why does this happen?\nAll summer, leaves are busy making food for the tree. To do this, they use a green material called chlorophyll. Chlorophyll uses sunlight to turn water and air into sugar. Because a leaf is so full of chlorophyll, it looks green.\nWhen fall comes, the days get shorter and colder. With less sunlight, the tree slows down and stops making new chlorophyll. As the green fades, other colors that were hiding in the leaf all along begin to show. Yellow and orange were there all summer, but the strong green covered them up.\nSome trees, like the red maple, do something more. In the fall they make a new red color in their leaves. Bright sunny days and cool nights make the reds stronger, so a sunny autumn brings the brightest colors.\nAt the same time, the tree grows a thin layer of cells where each leaf joins its branch. This layer slowly cuts the leaf off. Without water, the leaf dries, turns brown and falls to the ground. The tree rests through the winter, and in the spring it grows brand-new green leaves.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":5,"words":200,"onScreen":"Chart \"Colors in a leaf\": Green — made all summer, fades in fall. Yellow and orange — in the leaf all year, show in fall. Red — made new in fall, only in some trees.","extras":[{"k":"chart","title":"Colors in a leaf","lines":["Green — made all summer, fades in fall","Yellow and orange — in the leaf all year, show in fall","Red — made new in fall, only in some trees"]}]},"PF.R.L06.S.A":{"id":"PF.R.L06.S.A","title":"The Rocket Partners","text":"Yehuda and Binyamin were assigned to be partners for the science fair, and at first neither of them was happy about it.\nBinyamin had a new idea every five minutes. \"Let's build a volcano! No, a robot! No, a rocket that really flies!\" He sketched on napkins, on the backs of worksheets, and once even on his own arm.\nYehuda liked to finish one thing before starting the next. He kept a notebook with neat columns and wrote the date at the top of every page. When Binyamin suggested the rocket, Yehuda opened his notebook and asked, \"How will we measure how high it goes?\"\nBinyamin stopped. He had not thought about measuring.\nFor the next two weeks they argued, then tried, then argued again. Binyamin designed a rocket from a soda bottle, water and an air pump. Yehuda built a measuring pole from a broom handle, marked every ten centimeters, and made a chart to record each launch. Their first rocket wobbled and crashed after a few meters. Yehuda wrote it down. Binyamin added fins cut from a cereal box. The second rocket flew straight.\nOn the day of the fair, the judge, Rabbi Weiss, watched three launches. Then he picked up Yehuda's notebook and read every page. \"Whose idea were the fins?\" he asked.\n\"Mine,\" said Binyamin. \"But we only knew they worked because Yehuda measured.\"\nYehuda's ears turned pink. He had never thought of his notebook as anything special.\nThey won second place. On the way home, Binyamin was already sketching next year's project on the back of the ribbon. Yehuda reached into his bag and handed him a fresh notebook.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":6,"words":274},"PF.R.L06.S.B":{"id":"PF.R.L06.S.B","title":"The Shabbos Guest","text":"Every Friday afternoon, the Katz family hosted guests for Shabbos, and every Friday afternoon, Aharon and his younger brother Zev had the same jobs.\nAharon greeted the guests at the door. He could talk to anyone. Within a minute he knew where a guest was from, how long his trip had been, and whether he liked spicy food. Guests laughed at his jokes and forgot that they were strangers.\nZev set the table. He did not say much, but he noticed everything. He saw which guest stood stiffly by the bookshelf, and he quietly moved the name cards so that shy guests sat next to friendly ones.\nOne Friday, a guest named Mr. Adler arrived late, soaked from the rain. Aharon rushed to the door. \"Welcome! Where are you from? Did you drive? What do you do?\" Mr. Adler answered in single words and looked at the floor.\nZev brought a towel and a cup of hot tea and set them down without a word. Then he led Mr. Adler to a chair near the heater, next to their grandfather, who also spoke little.\nAt the meal, Mr. Adler was silent for a long time. But during the second course, Zaidy asked him a quiet question about the parsha, and Mr. Adler began to talk. He talked for half an hour.\nAfter Shabbos, Aharon found his brother clearing plates. \"How did you know he needed quiet?\" he asked.\nZev shrugged. \"He was tired. Not everybody wants questions right away.\"\nThe next Friday, when the doorbell rang, Aharon paused before opening the door. This time, he looked at the guest's face first.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":6,"words":271},"PF.R.L06.I.A":{"id":"PF.R.L06.I.A","title":"Should Our Yeshiva Start a Garden?","text":"Source 1: Gardens Grow Learners\nLearning by Doing. Students who grow a garden at school use math to measure beds and count seeds, science to test the soil and track how plants grow, and writing to keep a garden journal. Many teachers say lessons stick better when students can touch and see what they are learning. A boy who measures a garden bed is practicing the same skills he uses in a math lesson, but now the numbers really matter, because a bed that is too small will crowd the plants.\nHealthier Eating. Several studies have found that students in school garden programs become more willing to eat vegetables. When a boy grows a cucumber himself, he is far more willing to taste it.\nWorking Together. A garden needs many jobs: watering, weeding, building and harvesting. Students learn to share the work, and to keep going when a crop fails.\nSource 2: A letter from Mr. Kaplan, the building manager\nA garden is a wonderful idea, but we must plan for it. Raised beds, soil and tools would cost about $600. Someone must also water the plants during the summer, when the yeshiva is closed. I suggest that we start small, with two beds by the back door, and ask parents to sign up to water during the summer weeks. If the first year goes well, we can add more beds next spring. The best spot is the sunny patch by the back door, which gets light for most of the day and is close to a water tap. We do not need a shed; the tools can be kept in the basement.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":6,"words":273,"sources":2,"onScreen":"Source 1 has headings, a picture caption (\"Boys measure a new garden bed.\") and a box: \"Word to know — compost: old plant scraps that rot into rich soil.\" Source 2 is a letter.","extras":[{"k":"caption","text":"Boys measure a new garden bed.","src":1},{"k":"box","title":"Word to know","lines":["compost: old plant scraps that rot into rich soil."],"src":1}]},"PF.R.L06.I.B":{"id":"PF.R.L06.I.B","title":"Recess Before Lunch?","text":"Source 1: Play First, Then Eat\nLess Wasted Food. Some schools have moved recess to before lunch instead of after it. Studies at these schools have found that when students play first, they come to lunch hungry, eat more of their food, and throw less of it away. That means more of the nutrients in the meal end up helping them grow. At one school, the cafeteria workers noticed that the trash bins were much lighter after the change.\nCalmer Afternoons. Teachers at these schools say that students come back to class calmer and ready to learn. They are no longer gulping their food so they can rush outside, and they finish the day with more energy. Some teachers also noticed fewer arguments in the hallway, because the boys had already run off their extra energy before lunch.\nSource 2: A note from Rabbi Stern, the principal\nI like the idea of recess before lunch. But we must think about time. After recess, every boy needs to wash his hands, and boys who eat bread will also wash for netilas yadayim. With more than one hundred boys, that line takes time. If we make the change, lunch will need to start ten minutes later, and every class after lunch will move ten minutes later too. I would like to try it for one month before we decide. We would also need a teacher to watch the line at the sinks, and the lunchroom staff would need the new times before the first day.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":6,"words":253,"sources":2,"onScreen":"Source 1 has headings, a picture caption (\"Boys line up to wash before lunch.\") and a box: \"Word to know — nutrients: the parts of food your body needs to grow.\" Source 2 is a note.","extras":[{"k":"caption","text":"Boys line up to wash before lunch.","src":1},{"k":"box","title":"Word to know","lines":["nutrients: the parts of food your body needs to grow."],"src":1}]},"PF.R.L07.S.A":{"id":"PF.R.L07.S.A","title":"The Last Out","text":"Ezra had struck out four times in a row, and by the fifth inning of the yeshiva's big game against Darchei, he was dreading his next turn at bat. Each time he walked to the plate, he could feel the whole bench watching him. His bat felt like it weighed a hundred pounds.\nIn the last inning, the score was tied, with two outs and a runner on second base. Ezra was up. His friend Akiva leaned over the fence. \"Just watch the ball,\" he said. \"Forget the last four.\"\nThe first pitch whizzed past. Strike one. The second pitch was low, and Ezra let it go. Ball one. He took a slow breath, the way his rebbe had taught them to do before a hard test, and planted his feet.\nThe third pitch came straight down the middle. Ezra swung, and the ball sailed, not far, but just over the second baseman's glove. The runner raced home. The bench erupted. Boys jumped up and down and pounded on the fence.\nIn all the noise, Ezra noticed Shaul, the Darchei pitcher, standing alone on the mound with his head down. Two weeks earlier, Ezra had stood exactly like that after dropping an easy fly ball.\nAfter the handshakes, while his team celebrated, Ezra walked back to the mound. \"That was a great pitch,\" he told Shaul. \"You struck me out twice today. I just got lucky once.\"\nShaul looked up, surprised. \"You didn't look lucky. You looked calm.\"\n\"I wasn't,\" Ezra admitted. \"I just stopped thinking about the strikeouts.\"\nOn the bus ride home, Akiva asked why he had gone back to the mound when everyone else was cheering.\nEzra shrugged. \"Because I know how it feels to stand there,\" he said. \"Winning is fun. But it's better when nobody has to feel small because of it.\"","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":7,"words":307},"PF.R.L07.S.B":{"id":"PF.R.L07.S.B","title":"The Word He Missed","text":"For three weeks, Natan had studied the spelling list every night after supper. His older brother quizzed him in the car, and by the day of the yeshiva spelling bee, he could spell \"rhythm\" and \"necessary\" in his sleep.\nThe gym was packed. One by one, boys sat down after missing a word, until only Natan and a fifth grader named Boruch were left standing. Natan's heart drummed against his ribs.\n\"Boruch, your word is 'conscience,'\" said Rabbi Gordon, the judge. Boruch spelled it slowly and correctly.\n\"Natan, your word is 'privilege.'\"\nNatan swallowed and spelled it quickly. \"Correct,\" said Rabbi Gordon.\nThen Boruch got \"occasion\" and spelled it with two s's. The bell rang. Natan needed only one more word to win.\n\"Natan, your word is 'separate.'\"\nHe spelled it perfectly, and the room exploded with applause. As Rabbi Gordon handed him the trophy, a cold feeling crept up the back of Natan's neck. He had spelled \"privilege\" with a D in it, and the judge had not heard the mistake.\nThe trophy felt heavy and cold in his hands. He thought about Boruch, who had lost on a word he really had missed. Natan walked over to Rabbi Gordon and spoke quietly. \"Rebbi, I think I spelled 'privilege' wrong. I put a D in it.\"\nRabbi Gordon checked his notes, then looked at Natan for a long moment. \"You're right,\" he said. \"I missed it. Thank you for telling me.\"\nThe bee began again with just the two boys. This time Natan stumbled on \"occasion,\" and Boruch won.\nThat night, Natan's father found him in his room, staring at the empty spot on his shelf. \"Are you sorry you said something?\" his father asked.\nNatan thought about it for a while. \"No,\" he said at last. \"I would rather have an empty shelf than a trophy I didn't earn.\"","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":7,"words":311},"PF.R.L07.I.A":{"id":"PF.R.L07.I.A","title":"Every Boy Should Learn to Swim","text":"Swimming is not just a summer hobby. It is a skill that can save a life, and every boy should learn to swim before he finishes elementary school.\nThe first reason is safety. According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, drowning is a leading cause of death for children ages 1 to 14 in the United States. Many of these accidents happen in pools, lakes and at the beach, often only a short distance from safety. A child who can float, tread water and swim a short way has a much better chance of staying safe until help arrives.\nThe second reason is health. Swimming works almost every muscle in the body, strengthens the heart and lungs, and is gentle on the joints. Unlike some sports, it is something a person can keep doing for his whole life. Many grandfathers who can no longer run still swim laps every morning.\nSome people say that swimming lessons cost too much, or that there are no separate swim times in their neighborhood. These are real problems, but they can be solved. Community centers often offer group lessons at a low price, and many have set hours for boys and men. A yeshiva can also arrange a block of lessons for a whole class, which brings the cost down for every family.\nFinally, learning to swim builds confidence. A boy who was once afraid of deep water and then learns to swim the length of a pool discovers that he can do hard things. That lesson follows him back to the classroom.\nFamilies celebrate a boy's first siyum and his first time leining. Learning to swim deserves a place on that list. It protects him, keeps him healthy, and teaches him that fear can be beaten. Parents and schools should make sure that no boy grows up without it.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":7,"words":308,"genre":"argument"},"PF.R.L07.I.B":{"id":"PF.R.L07.I.B","title":"Start the Day by Moving","text":"Most mornings, boys arrive at yeshiva, sit down for davening, and then sit for hours more. A short burst of exercise before the first class would help them learn better, and every yeshiva should add ten minutes of movement to the start of the day.\nThe first reason is focus. Researchers who study learning have found that even a short session of exercise can improve attention for a while afterward. When boys move first, their minds are more ready to sit still and concentrate on a hard sugya. Many teachers notice the same thing after recess.\nThe second reason is health. Health experts recommend that children be physically active for about an hour every day, and many children get far less than that. Ten minutes every morning would not solve the whole problem, but it would add almost an hour of activity over a school week.\nSome people worry that exercise will take time away from learning. But ten minutes is a small price if the rest of the morning goes better. Teachers already spend many minutes each day calming restless boys and repeating directions, and a class that has already moved may need less of that. The exercise could also be simple, such as jumping jacks, stretching or a quick walk around the building, so it needs no special room or equipment.\nFinally, a shared morning routine builds a sense of team. When a whole grade does the same exercises together, older boys can lead younger ones, and everyone starts the day as part of one group. It also gives new boys an easy way to belong.\nOur yeshiva already teaches that the body is a gift that we must care for. Ten minutes of exercise each morning is a simple way to live that lesson. The menahel and the teachers should try it for one month and see the difference for themselves.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":7,"words":314,"genre":"argument"},"PF.R.L08.S.A":{"id":"PF.R.L08.S.A","title":"The Chavrusa","text":"When Rebbi announced that I would be learning with the new boy, Reuven, my stomach sank like a stone in a pond. I had spent all summer hoping to be paired with my best friend, Yitzy. Reuven had just moved from Yerushalayim, and his English was halting. I was sure our chavrusa time would be one long, awkward silence.\nThe first day proved me half right. Reuven read the Gemara aloud in a quick, singing voice, and I stumbled along behind him, embarrassed. When we reached a hard Tosafos, I suggested that we skip it and come back later.\nReuven frowned. \"Skip?\" he said. \"In my old yeshiva, the hard part is the good part. It is where the treasure is.\"\nI didn't want to admit it, but that stung. For the rest of the week, I watched him. He didn't seem to mind being stuck. He would read a line, push his chair back, stare at the ceiling, and then, sometimes after ten minutes, snap his fingers and laugh. Slowly I saw that what I had taken for showing off was something else: he simply loved the struggle.\nBy the second week, I was trying it too. When we hit a wall, instead of turning the page, we argued. My arguments were clumsy at first, and Reuven corrected my reading more times than I could count. But one afternoon, I asked a question that made him stop, flip back three pages, and say, \"Dovid, that is a real kasha.\" He looked at me as if he were seeing me for the first time.\nLater, at recess, Yitzy asked whether I was still stuck with \"the quiet kid.\"\n\"He's not quiet,\" I said, surprised to hear myself defend him. \"He's just quiet in English. In Gemara, he never stops talking.\"\nMonths later, Reuven told me that he had been terrified on that first day. He had thought I was bored with him and wished for another partner. \"You looked at the Tosafos like it was a mountain,\" he said. \"I thought you looked at me the same way.\"\nI laughed, because he was right on both counts. The mountain is still there. But now we climb it together.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":8,"words":368},"PF.R.L08.S.B":{"id":"PF.R.L08.S.B","title":"Zaidy's Radio","text":"When Zaidy moved in with us, he brought exactly three boxes and one broken radio. The radio was older than my father: a wooden box with a cracked dial and a cloth front that smelled like dust. Zaidy set it on the kitchen counter as if it were made of gold.\n\"It doesn't work,\" I pointed out.\n\"Not yet,\" said Zaidy.\nTo me, the radio was a relic, a leftover from a world that had already been thrown away. We had a speaker the size of a soda can that could play almost anything ever recorded. Why would anyone bother fixing a box that could barely pick up one station?\nBut every evening after Maariv, Zaidy sat at the counter with a tiny screwdriver, a magnifying glass and a pile of parts he had ordered from a catalog. At first I only watched from the doorway. Then he asked me to hold the flashlight. Then he asked me to read the numbers printed on the tiny tubes and wires, because his eyes were tired.\nSome nights nothing happened at all. Zaidy would test a part, shake his head and set it aside. I would have quit after the first week. Zaidy just said, \"Good. Now we know one more thing it isn't.\"\nSlowly, I started to understand the radio: not only its parts, but the patience it took. Each wire had a reason. Each evening that seemed wasted crossed one more problem off the list.\nThen, one night in late winter, Zaidy turned the dial and the kitchen filled with a crackle, a hum, and then a voice reading the weather. My father came running in from the den. Zaidy didn't cheer. He just closed his eyes and listened, smiling.\nLater, he told me why the radio mattered. It had sat on his own father's kitchen counter, and he had listened to it as a boy, the night the family heard that the war was over. \"I didn't fix it to hear the weather,\" he said. \"I fixed it so that you would know where you come from.\"\nI had thought we were repairing a machine. Zaidy had been repairing something else the whole time.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":8,"words":365},"PF.R.L08.I.A":{"id":"PF.R.L08.I.A","title":"The Erie Canal","text":"Source 1: The Canal That Built a City\nBefore 1825, moving goods from the Great Lakes to New York City was slow and expensive. Wagons crawled over rough roads, and shipping a ton of flour could cost about $100. Many people had called the plan \"Clinton's Ditch,\" after Governor DeWitt Clinton, and said it would never work. Then the Erie Canal opened. The canal stretched 363 miles across New York State, from Albany on the Hudson River to Buffalo on Lake Erie. Mules walking on a towpath pulled flat boats loaded with grain, lumber and salt.\nThe results came quickly. Shipping costs dropped to about $10 a ton, and trips that had taken weeks now took days. Because goods could flow cheaply from the Midwest to the coast, farmers in Ohio and Michigan could sell their crops in the East. Towns such as Syracuse, Rochester and Buffalo grew up along the canal. New York City, at the end of the route, became the busiest port in the country. Even today, the canal is used by pleasure boats, and its old towpath has become a trail for walking and biking.\nSource 2: Hands That Dug the Canal (from a museum guide)\nThe Erie Canal is often called an engineering wonder, and it was. But it was also built almost entirely by hand. Thousands of workers, many of them immigrants who had just arrived from Ireland, cleared forests, dug through clay and blasted rock with black powder. They used shovels, picks and wheelbarrows, and they earned less than a dollar a day. The work was dangerous. Many workers fell ill in the swamps near Syracuse, and accidents with explosives were common. Doctors at the time did not understand that mosquitoes carried the fever. Where the land rose, the workers also built locks out of heavy stone.\nWhen the canal opened, many of these workers stayed. They settled in the new canal towns and found jobs on the boats and at the locks, the gates that raise and lower boats from one water level to the next. Their families helped build the communities that grew up along the water, and many of their grandchildren still live in those towns today.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":8,"words":367,"sources":2},"PF.R.L08.I.B":{"id":"PF.R.L08.I.B","title":"The Railroad Across the Country","text":"Source 1: Coast to Coast in a Week\nBefore 1869, crossing the United States was a long and dangerous trip. A family traveling by covered wagon from Missouri to California could spend four to six months on the trail. Sending goods by ship around South America took even longer. Many travelers never reached the end of the trail at all.\nIn 1869, that changed. Two railroad companies, one building east from California and one building west from the middle of the country, met at Promontory Summit in Utah. Building through the mountains of the West was the hardest part of the job. When the two lines finally met, workers hammered in a last golden spike, and the first railroad across the continent was complete. The news was sent across the country by telegraph, and cities held celebrations.\nThe effects were enormous. A trip from New York to San Francisco now took about a week. Mail, newspapers and goods crossed the country quickly, and businesses could sell their products from coast to coast. New towns sprang up along the tracks, many of them built around train stations. Some of those towns are still busy cities today.\nSource 2: The Mountain Builders (from a history museum display)\nThe railroad's hardest stretch was the climb through the Sierra Nevada mountains in California. Here the Central Pacific company hired thousands of workers who had come from China, and they soon made up most of the company's workforce. Using hand drills, hammers and black powder, they carved tunnels through solid granite, sometimes moving forward only a foot a day. They often worked in small teams, sharing tools and cooking their own meals in camp. Winters brought deep snow and avalanches, and many workers lost their lives. In one winter, snow buried some camps so deeply that workers dug tunnels through the snow just to reach their tunnels in the rock. The Chinese workers were paid less than other workers for the same jobs. Yet their skill and endurance made it possible to finish the line years earlier than many experts had expected. Today, historians agree that the railroad could not have been completed when it was without them.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":8,"words":363,"sources":2},"PF.R.L09.S.A":{"id":"PF.R.L09.S.A","title":"Zaidy Hersh's Watch","text":"On the morning of his bar mitzvah, Avraham stood in front of the mirror and fastened his grandfather's watch around his wrist. The leather band was soft with age, and the gold case had a scratch shaped like a crescent moon. He held it to his ear. It was ticking.\nThree years earlier, it had not been.\nHe was ten the summer Zaidy Hersh got sick. The watch had stopped the same week, and it sat on the nightstand beside Zaidy's bed like a small, silent guest. Avraham visited every afternoon. Zaidy was too weak to sit up for long, but his hands were still steady, and one day he asked for the watch and a tiny screwdriver.\n\"A watchmaker for forty years,\" he said, \"and I let my own watch sit there dead? Come. Hold the light.\"\nFor six weeks, they worked on it together. Zaidy explained each part as if it were a character in a story: the mainspring that stored the energy, the balance wheel that kept the rhythm, the tiny jewels that let everything turn without wearing away. Some days he could only talk, and Avraham did the work while Zaidy watched and corrected him. \"Gently,\" he would say. \"A watch doesn't like to be rushed. Neither do people.\" Avraham learned to hold his breath while setting a tiny screw, and to tap the case lightly to hear whether a part was loose.\nOn the last day of summer, Avraham fitted the final screw. The second hand jumped, hesitated, and then began to sweep. Zaidy laughed, a weak but happy sound. \"Now you know how it works,\" he said. \"Keep it running for me.\"\nZaidy passed away that winter. At the shiva, Avraham sat quietly in a corner with the watch in his pocket, listening to it tick.\nNow, standing at the mirror, Avraham felt the familiar weight on his wrist. He had wound the watch every single morning for three years. Twice it had stopped, and twice he had opened the case and fixed it himself, hearing Zaidy's voice in his head: Gently. His father knocked on the door and told him it was time to leave for shul.\nIn shul, when he was called up to the Torah, his hands shook as he took hold of the wooden handles. He glanced down at the watch. The second hand was sweeping, steady and calm, as if nothing in the world could rush it.\nHe took a breath, found the place, and began to read.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":9,"words":419},"PF.R.L09.S.B":{"id":"PF.R.L09.S.B","title":"The Letter in the Sefer","text":"Moshe was looking for a Chumash with big print for his little brother when he found the old book on the top shelf of the shul library. Its cover was cracked, and the spine was held together with tape that had turned yellow. When he opened it, a folded paper slipped out and drifted to the floor. The library smelled of old paper and lemon polish.\nIt was a letter, written in careful, slanting English. The date at the top was 1948.\nMy dear son Shloime, it began. I am writing this on the ship, so that you will have it if I do not reach America. This sefer is the only thing I carried out of our town. Everything else was lost.\nMoshe sat down on the library steps. Shloime. That was his great-grandfather's name.\nWhen I was your age, I did not like to learn. I wanted to be outside, running with the other boys. My father did not shout at me. Each night he simply sat beside me with this sefer, one line at a time, until one night I realized that I was waiting for him to come.\nMoshe thought of his own father, who learned with him every night after supper, even when Moshe sighed and watched the clock.\nIf you are reading this, then I reached America and gave it to you myself. If I did not, then someone kind has found it. Either way, do not let it sit on a shelf. A sefer is not saved by keeping it safe. It is saved by being learned.\nThe letter was signed simply: Your loving father, Eliezer.\nMoshe carried the book home that evening, holding it with both hands. At supper, he unfolded the letter and read it aloud. His little brother, who usually could not sit still, listened without moving. When he finished, no one spoke for a long time. Then his father reached over and gently touched the cracked cover.\n\"My Zaidy used to talk about a sefer that came over on the ship,\" he said quietly. \"We always thought it was lost.\" He turned the pages slowly, as if they might crumble.\nThat night, after the dishes were done, Moshe didn't wait to be called. He carried the old sefer to the table, set it down between himself and his father, and opened it to the first page.\n\"One line at a time?\" his father asked.\n\"One line at a time,\" said Moshe.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":9,"words":412},"PF.R.L09.I.A":{"id":"PF.R.L09.I.A","title":"A Water Station for the Yeshiva?","text":"Source 1: A letter from the Student Council\nTo the Menahel and the Parent Board:\nThe Student Council asks the yeshiva to install a water-bottle filling station in the main hallway. Right now, many boys buy plastic bottles of water from the vending machine every day. A filling station would let them refill the same bottle, saving money and cutting down on plastic waste.\nFor one week, we counted the empty bottles in the recycling bins: there were 412. That is more than 14,000 bottles over a school year. A filling station would also give boys cold, filtered water, which would encourage them to drink more water and fewer sugary drinks. A refill station also uses less energy than making and shipping new bottles. Other schools in Baltimore that installed stations report that boys now carry reusable bottles proudly. The station costs about $900, and the Parent Board has already offered to pay half.\nOur hallway is also very hot in September and June. Last year, the air conditioner in the beis medrash broke twice.\nWe hope the yeshiva will make this change, for the health of the boys and for the world that Hashem gave us.\nSource 2: Quick-Start Guide for the AquaFill filling station\n1. Turn off the water supply at the valve below the sink.\n2. Mount the station on the wall using the four bolts provided.\n3. Connect the water line to the station and tighten it by hand.\n4. Turn the water supply back on and check for leaks.\n5. Plug in the station and wait 30 minutes for the water to cool.\n6. Press the button and run water for 2 minutes to flush the filter before the first use.\nNote: Do not install the station above an electrical outlet.\nThe counter on the front shows how many plastic bottles you have saved. Replace the filter when the light turns red.\nSource 3: City Water Department fact sheet\nOur city's tap water is tested more than 500 times every month and meets all federal safety standards. A reusable bottle refilled with tap water costs less than a penny per refill. Filtering is not needed for safety, but some people prefer the taste of filtered water. Plastic water bottles can take hundreds of years to break down in a landfill, and many are never recycled. The department recommends that schools and offices provide places to refill bottles with tap water.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":9,"words":405,"sources":3},"PF.R.L09.I.B":{"id":"PF.R.L09.I.B","title":"A 3D Printer for the Science Room?","text":"Source 1: A letter from the Science Club\nTo the Menahel:\nThe Science Club asks the yeshiva to buy a 3D printer for the science room. A 3D printer builds solid objects layer by layer from a design made on a computer. With one, boys could design and print models of molecules, parts for robotics projects, and replacement pieces for broken classroom equipment. A printer for a classroom costs about $400, and the plastic for a whole year would cost about $60.\nEngineers and doctors use 3D printing every day, from making airplane parts to building custom braces. Boys who learn to design for a 3D printer will be learning a skill used in many careers. Several schools in our area already have printers, and their students won prizes at last year's science fair. Last spring, two boys from our club used the library's printer to make a replacement knob for the science room's microscope.\nOur club also raised $350 at the Chanukah bake sale, which could pay for part of the cost. Last month, the club went on a trip to the planetarium, and everyone enjoyed the show.\nWe hope the yeshiva will help us bring this tool into our classroom.\nSource 2: Public library technology guide\nThe Central Library offers free 3D printing for students. A small object such as a key chain usually uses less than $1 of plastic and takes 30 to 60 minutes to print. Library staff note that most beginners' prints fail at least once, so students should plan time to try again. Students must sign up in advance, and each student may print one object per week. Library staff can help beginners prepare their designs.\nSource 3: Getting Started with the PrintPro Mini\n1. Place the printer on a flat, sturdy table away from drafts.\n2. Remove the tape that holds the print bed in place.\n3. Load the spool of plastic onto the holder and feed the end into the nozzle.\n4. Turn on the printer and let the nozzle heat up until the light turns green.\n5. Insert the memory card with your design and select \"Print.\"\n6. When the print is finished, wait 10 minutes for the bed to cool before removing the object.\nNever touch the nozzle while it is hot. Clean the print bed with a dry cloth after each use. If the plastic stops coming out of the nozzle, turn the printer off and let it cool before checking for a jam.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":9,"words":415,"sources":3},"PF.R.L05.I2.A":{"id":"PF.R.L05.I2.A","title":"From Wheat Field to Challah","text":"Every Friday, challah sits on the Shabbos table. But before it was dough, it was a plant growing in a field far away. Turning wheat into bread takes many steps and many people.\nFirst, a farmer plants wheat seeds. Most wheat in our country is winter wheat. The seeds are planted in the fall, sprout a little, and rest under the snow. When spring comes, the plants grow tall and green.\nNext, the plants turn golden and dry. Each stalk now has a head at the top, packed with small seeds called kernels. A huge machine called a combine drives through the field. It cuts the stalks and shakes the kernels loose, all at the same time.\nThen trucks carry the kernels to a mill. At the mill, rollers crush the kernels into fine white powder. That powder is flour. It is sifted, packed into bags, and sent to stores and bakeries.\nFinally, the flour reaches a kitchen. A baker mixes it with water, yeast, eggs, oil, a little sugar and salt. The dough rises, gets braided, and bakes until it is shiny and brown.\nSo the next time you make Hamotzi, remember the farmer, the miller and the baker who all helped bring that challah to your table.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":5,"words":209,"onScreen":"Box beside the article — \"Wheat facts\": winter wheat is planted in the fall and harvested in early summer; one bushel of wheat weighs about 60 pounds.","extras":[{"k":"box","title":"Wheat facts","lines":["winter wheat is planted in the fall and harvested in early summer","one bushel of wheat weighs about 60 pounds"]}]},"PF.R.L05.I2.B":{"id":"PF.R.L05.I2.B","title":"From Maple Tree to Syrup","text":"Have you ever poured maple syrup on pancakes? That sweet syrup started as sap inside a tree. Turning sap into syrup takes hard work and a lot of patience.\nFirst, the farmer waits for the right weather. Sap runs best at the end of winter, when nights are below freezing and days are warm and sunny. That is when sugar maple trees start pushing sap up from their roots.\nNext, the farmer taps the trees. He drills a small hole into each trunk and pushes in a spout. Clear, watery sap drips out into a bucket or flows through plastic tubes to a big tank.\nThen the sap is boiled. Sap is mostly water, with only a little sugar. In a building called a sugarhouse, the sap boils for hours in a wide, flat pan. Steam pours out of the roof as the water boils away, and the sap slowly turns thick and golden.\nFinally, the syrup is strained to take out any grit and poured into bottles while it is still hot.\nIt takes a lot of sap to make a little syrup. The next time you taste it, think of all the steps that went into every drop.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":5,"words":200,"onScreen":"Box beside the article — \"Sap to syrup\": about 40 gallons of sap make 1 gallon of syrup; sap season lasts about 4 to 6 weeks.","extras":[{"k":"box","title":"Sap to syrup","lines":["about 40 gallons of sap make 1 gallon of syrup","sap season lasts about 4 to 6 weeks"]}]},"PF.R.L06.I2.A":{"id":"PF.R.L06.I2.A","title":"Riding a Bike to School","text":"Source 1: More Kids on Bikes\nFifty years ago, many children rode bikes to school. Today far fewer do. Parents worry about traffic, and many families simply drive. Some towns are trying to change that.\nWhy bike? First, riding is good exercise. A boy who bikes ten minutes each way gets twenty minutes of activity before supper, without a gym or a coach. Second, biking helps the whole neighborhood. Every boy on a bike is one less car in the drop-off line, which means less traffic and cleaner air near the school. Third, many riders say they feel awake and ready to learn when they arrive.\nSome towns paint bike lanes on busy streets and add bike racks near the school doors. In one town, the number of students biking more than doubled in two years after new lanes were built.\nSource 2: Safe Riding Rules\nBefore you ride:\n1. Put on a helmet that fits snugly and buckle the strap.\n2. Check that your tires are full and your brakes work.\n3. Plan your route with a parent, using quiet streets or bike lanes.\nOn the road:\n4. Ride on the right side, in the same direction as cars.\n5. Stop at every stop sign and look left, right and left again.\n6. Use hand signals before you turn.\nAt school:\n7. Lock your bike to the rack, through the frame and a wheel.\nRemember: a helmet only protects you if you wear it every single ride, even on short trips.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":6,"words":252,"sources":2,"onScreen":"Source 1 is an article with a heading. Source 2 is a numbered list of rules with three small headings."},"PF.R.L06.I2.B":{"id":"PF.R.L06.I2.B","title":"A Class Fish Tank","text":"Source 1: Why a Fish Tank Belongs in the Classroom\nMore and more teachers are setting up fish tanks in their classrooms, and there are good reasons for it.\nFirst, a tank teaches responsibility. Boys take turns feeding the fish and checking the water, and they learn that living things depend on them every single day. Second, a tank is a science lesson that never ends. Students can watch how fish eat, swim and rest, and how plants in the water grow toward the light. Third, many teachers say a fish tank makes a classroom calmer. Watching fish glide back and forth seems to help some boys settle down after recess.\nA tank does take work, but most classes find it is worth the effort. One fourth-grade class even kept a journal about its fish for the whole year, and by June the boys could tell each fish apart by the way it swam.\nSource 2: Fish Tank Jobs\nEvery day:\n1. Feed the fish a small pinch of food, only as much as they finish in two minutes.\n2. Check that the filter is running and the water looks clear.\nEvery week:\n3. Wipe the inside glass with a clean sponge.\n4. Take out about one fourth of the water and replace it with clean water that has been treated.\nEvery month:\n5. Rinse the filter sponge in old tank water, not tap water.\nRemember: too much food is the most common mistake. Leftover food makes the water dirty and can make fish sick.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":6,"words":255,"sources":2,"onScreen":"Source 1 is an article with a heading. Source 2 is a numbered list of jobs with three small headings."},"PF.R.L07.I2.A":{"id":"PF.R.L07.I2.A","title":"Saving the Leaning Tower","text":"The Leaning Tower of Pisa, in Italy, is one of the most famous buildings in the world, and it is famous for a mistake. It was meant to stand straight beside the city's great cathedral, as a home for its bells. Builders began the bell tower in 1173. By the time they had finished the third floor, the tower had already started to tip. The problem was underground: the tower stood on soft, damp soil made of clay and sand, and one side sank faster than the other.\nWork stopped for almost a hundred years, partly because of wars. Surprisingly, the long pause helped. The soil had time to settle and grow firmer, so the tower did not collapse when building began again. The builders even tried to fix the lean by making the upper floors a little taller on the low side, which is why the tower curves slightly, like a banana. The bell chamber at the top was finally finished nearly two hundred years after work began.\nOver the centuries, the lean kept getting worse. By 1990, engineers feared the tower could fall, and it was closed to visitors. Some people suggested braces, cables or even heavy weights. Their solution was clever and careful. Instead of pushing the tower, they slowly removed small amounts of soil from under the high side, a little at a time. As the ground there sank, the tower eased back by about a foot and a half.\nIn 2001 the tower reopened. Engineers believe it should now stay stable for at least two hundred years. Its story shows that a mistake can be fixed, but only with patience, careful measuring and respect for the forces of nature. The tower still leans, of course, which is exactly why millions of people come to see it.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":7,"words":301},"PF.R.L07.I2.B":{"id":"PF.R.L07.I2.B","title":"The Family Who Built the Brooklyn Bridge","text":"When the Brooklyn Bridge opened in 1883, it was the longest suspension bridge in the world. Crowds cheered as fireworks lit up the East River. Many had doubted that a bridge so long could hang from cables at all. But few people in that crowd knew how much one family had given to build it.\nThe bridge was designed by an engineer named John Roebling. In 1869, before construction had even begun, his foot was crushed by a ferry while he was surveying the site. He died a few weeks later. His son, Washington Roebling, also an engineer, took over the project.\nWashington worked side by side with his men. To build the bridge's two great towers, workers dug deep under the river inside huge wooden boxes called caissons, filled with pressed air to keep the water out. Coming up too quickly from the caissons made many workers terribly sick. Washington himself was struck by this illness and became too weak to visit the bridge.\nThat is when Washington Roebling showed what he was made of. Too sick to walk to the river, he moved into a house with a view of the bridge. He studied every drawing, wrote long, exact instructions each day, and sent them to his team of assistant engineers, who carried them out on the site. For years, the chief engineer led the busiest building site in America from his bedroom. He watched every step of the work through a telescope from his window.\nToday the bridge carries thousands of cars and walkers every day. A plaque on the bridge honors the Roeblings and the workers who built it. Historians agree that without Washington's steady leadership from that window, the bridge might never have been finished. The Brooklyn Bridge is a triumph of steel and stone, but it is also a story of a family that refused to quit.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":7,"words":313},"PF.R.L08.S2.A":{"id":"PF.R.L08.S2.A","title":"The Last Game of the Tournament","text":"The chess club met in the yeshiva library every Tuesday, but the tournament was held in a big hall across town, and it felt like another planet. Rows of tables stretched to the far wall. Clocks ticked. Nobody talked. My hands were ice.\nI had won my first three games. One more win and our school would take first place. My opponent, a tall boy from Cleveland named Shloimy, looked about as calm as a sleeping cat.\n\"You're up against the champion,\" whispered my friend Yitzy as I sat down. That did not help.\nWe set up the pieces. Shloimy pushed his king's pawn forward two squares without even looking up, as if he had played this opening a thousand times. Maybe he had. I answered the way Rabbi Gross had drilled us, move after move, and slowly my breathing settled.\nFor forty minutes we traded pieces. Then I saw it: a chance to trap his queen. My heart galloped. I reached for my knight, and then I stopped. Rabbi Gross, our coach, always said, \"Before you grab, check what he can grab.\" I looked again. If I moved the knight, Shloimy could check my king and win my rook. The trap was a trap for me.\nI sat on my hands and thought. The clock ticked louder and louder. Finally, I made a quiet move, a pawn one square forward, protecting my king. Shloimy frowned. For the first time all day, he leaned back and stared at the board for a long time.\nTwenty minutes later, he offered a draw. I looked at the board, at the clock, at Yitzy pressing his face against the window. A draw meant our school tied for first, not alone in first. I shook Shloimy's hand.\nOn the bus home, Rabbi Gross sat next to me. \"I watched you not grab the knight,\" he said. \"That was the best move you made today, and it isn't even on the score sheet.\"\nI didn't win the tournament. But I finally understood what he had been trying to teach us all year.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":8,"words":347},"PF.R.L08.S2.B":{"id":"PF.R.L08.S2.B","title":"The Canoe","text":"Zaidy's canoe had been hanging in the garage for twelve years, upside down, under a blanket of dust. When I asked if we could take it out on the lake, My uncle Moshe laughed. \"That boat is older than your father,\" he said. \"It leaks like a basket.\"\nZaidy didn't laugh. He climbed the ladder, wiped the dust off with his sleeve, and ran his hand along the cracked wood. \"Then we'll fix it,\" he said.\nWe lifted it down together. It was heavier than I expected, and underneath it was a spider web the size of a pillow. Zaidy turned the canoe over and tapped the bottom with his knuckles, listening, the way a doctor listens to a heartbeat. Then he nodded, as if the boat had answered him.\nFor the next three weeks, every afternoon after yeshiva, I went to Zaidy's garage. We sanded the old paint until our arms ached. We filled the cracks, one by one, with a thick paste that smelled terrible. Zaidy was meticulous. If a patch was even slightly bumpy, he sanded it again. I was impatient. \"It's fine, Zaidy,\" I said more than once. \"No one will see that part.\"\n\"The water will see it,\" he answered.\nAt last we painted the canoe dark green, and on the last day, Zaidy let me paint its name on the side in white letters: Bracha.\nThe morning we carried it to the lake, my cousins came to watch. I could tell they were expecting it to sink. I held my breath as we pushed off. The canoe sat on the water like a duck on a pond, steady and dry. Zaidy paddled in the back, humming. I paddled in the front, grinning so hard my face hurt.\nIn the middle of the lake, Zaidy stopped paddling. \"Your uncle thinks I fixed this boat for you,\" he said. \"But really, you fixed it for me. I haven't been out here since my brother passed away.\"\nI didn't know what to say, so I kept paddling, slowly, so the morning would last.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":8,"words":346},"PF.R.L09.I2.A":{"id":"PF.R.L09.I2.A","title":"A Proposal: Composting in the Lunchroom","text":"To the Principal, from the Grade 8 Science Committee\nEvery day, our lunchroom throws away two full bags of food scraps: apple cores, banana peels, crusts and lettuce. All of it goes to the landfill. We propose a different path for that waste. With a simple composting program, the scraps could become rich soil for the yeshiva's flower beds and the new vegetable garden.\nComposting is not complicated. Tiny living things in soil, such as bacteria and worms, break down food scraps into a dark, crumbly material called compost. Gardeners call it \"black gold\" because it helps plants grow strong.\nOur committee also visited a public school two towns away that has composted for three years. Their principal told us the program now runs almost by itself: younger students learn the rules from older ones, and the school's garden produces enough vegetables to share with a local food pantry each fall. Their custodian said the compost area takes him about ten minutes a week. We were impressed by how simple their system was, and we copied many of its rules in the plan below.\nWe tested the idea for two weeks. Our class sorted its own lunch scraps and weighed them each day. We collected about 15 pounds of fruit and vegetable scraps per week from just one class. If the whole school took part, the amount kept out of the landfill would be far larger.\nHow the program would work:\n1. Place a green bin, marked \"Fruit and Vegetable Scraps Only,\" next to each trash can.\n2. Train lunch monitors to remind boys what goes in: no meat, no dairy, no wrappers.\n3. At the end of each day, monitors carry the bins to the compost box behind the garden.\n4. Add a layer of dry leaves on top of each load to keep odors down.\n5. Turn the pile with a pitchfork once a week so air reaches the inside.\n6. After about three months, sift out the finished compost and spread it on the garden beds.\nThe program would cost about $150 for bins and a compost box. Some may worry about smells or flies, but a pile covered with leaves and turned weekly stays clean. We believe this small step can teach responsibility, help our garden, and show that every boy can make a difference.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":9,"words":389},"PF.R.L09.I2.B":{"id":"PF.R.L09.I2.B","title":"A Proposal: Rain Barrels for the Yeshiva Garden","text":"To the Building Committee, from the Grade 8 Garden Club\nLast summer, our garden club watered the yeshiva's vegetable beds with a hose almost every day. Our water bill for July was the highest of the year. Meanwhile, every time it rained, water poured off the roof, ran down the driveway and disappeared into the storm drain. We propose catching that rain in barrels and using it in the garden.\nA rain barrel is a large container placed under a downspout, the pipe that carries rain from the roof gutter to the ground. It has a lid with a screen to keep out leaves and insects, and a spigot near the bottom for filling watering cans. A roof collects far more water than most people expect: even a small storm can fill a barrel.\nWe researched the idea at the town library and on a website run by our county's water department. The county encourages rain barrels and explains how to set them up safely. According to the county, a roof the size of a small house can collect hundreds of gallons in a single heavy rain, far more than a garden of our size needs in a week. We also spoke with a neighbor who has used two barrels for five summers. He told us that his barrels were full almost every time he went out to water, and that he had not needed his hose for the garden since the first year.\nHow to install a rain barrel:\n1. Choose a downspout near the garden, on level ground.\n2. Build a sturdy base of cinder blocks so the barrel sits about a foot off the ground.\n3. Cut the downspout just above the top of the barrel.\n4. Attach a short bendable elbow so the water flows into the screened lid.\n5. Set the barrel on the base and make sure it does not wobble.\n6. Attach the overflow hose so extra water flows away from the building.\nTwo barrels would cost about $200. Some may worry about mosquitoes, but a tight screened lid keeps them out. Using rainwater would lower our water bill, keep runoff out of the street, and teach younger boys how to care for the world Hashem gave us.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":9,"words":375},"PF.R.L05.S2.A":{"id":"PF.R.L05.S2.A","title":"The Vase","text":"I was supposed to be watching my little brother Shmuli. Abba had gone next door for ten minutes, and I was on the couch reading my new book. Shmuli was bouncing a ball in the living room, even though we are not allowed to play ball inside.\nThen I heard a crash.\nAbba's blue vase lay on the floor in three pieces. It was the vase Zeidy had brought from Eretz Yisroel. Shmuli's face turned white, and his lip started to shake.\n\"Don't tell,\" he begged. \"Please, Chezky. We can say the cat did it.\"\nFor a second I thought about it. We do have a cat, and it does jump on tables. But I also knew that I had been reading instead of watching him. Part of this was my fault, too.\nWhen Abba came home, I was holding the pieces. \"Abba,\" I said, \"the vase broke. Shmuli was playing ball inside, and I was supposed to stop him, but I wasn't paying attention.\"\nAbba looked at the pieces for a long time. Then he sighed and hugged us both. \"I'm sad about the vase,\" he said, \"but I'm proud that you told me the truth. A vase can be glued. Trust is much harder to fix.\"\nThat night, the three of us glued the vase together. You can still see the cracks, but I don't mind. Every time I see them, I remember that night.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":5,"words":237},"PF.R.L05.S2.B":{"id":"PF.R.L05.S2.B","title":"Zeidy Learns to Text","text":"When Zeidy got his first smartphone, he held it the way you would hold a bird that might fly away.\n\"Avi,\" he said, \"you will be my teacher.\"\nI thought it would take five minutes. It took three weeks.\nOn the first day, Zeidy pressed the screen so hard that he opened six apps at once. On the second day, he sent Uncle Shimon a message that said only \"kkkkkkkkk.\" On the fourth day, he called the fire department by mistake and had to apologize to a very patient man.\nI wanted to grab the phone and do it all for him. My hands were itching. But I remembered how Zeidy taught me to tie my shoes when I was little. He never once tied them for me. He just showed me again and again, and he never sounded tired of it.\nSo I took a breath and showed him again. And again. And again.\nBy the third week, Zeidy could send a message, take a picture, and call me without calling anyone else first. On Friday afternoon, my phone buzzed. It was a picture of Zeidy's challah, fresh from the oven, with a message underneath: \"Good Shabbos, my teacher. Thank you for not giving up on me.\"\nI showed it to Abba, and I could not stop smiling. Teaching Zeidy was slow, but it turned out to be one of the best things I ever did.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":5,"words":237},"PF.R.L06.S2.A":{"id":"PF.R.L06.S2.A","title":"The Sukkah Lights","text":"Every year, my brothers Shimon and Eli hung the lights in our sukkah, and every year it ended in an argument.\nShimon was fifteen and did everything slowly. Before he hammered a single nail, he measured the walls, drew a plan on graph paper, and counted the bulbs twice. Eli was ten and did everything fast. He liked to grab the first string of lights he saw and start climbing.\nThis year was no different. \"We don't need a plan,\" Eli said, already halfway up the ladder. \"It's lights. You plug them in and they shine.\"\nShimon frowned. \"If you start at that corner, the cord won't reach the outlet.\"\nEli ignored him and kept stringing. Twenty minutes later, the lights stretched beautifully across three walls and stopped a full arm's length from the outlet. Eli stared at the dangling plug as if it had betrayed him.\nI expected Shimon to say \"I told you so.\" Instead, he walked over, looked at the cord, and laughed. \"You did the hard part,\" he said. \"The walls look great. Let's just turn it around.\" Together they unhooked the strand and started from the other corner, Shimon reading the plan out loud and Eli climbing, quick as a squirrel.\nWhen Abba came out at night, the sukkah glowed like a lantern. \"Who did this?\" he asked.\nEli pointed at Shimon. Shimon pointed at Eli. For once, they were both right.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":6,"words":237},"PF.R.L06.S2.B":{"id":"PF.R.L06.S2.B","title":"The New Boy","text":"The new boy, Ephraim, stood in the doorway of the classroom as if the floor might crack under him. He had moved from Toronto in the middle of the year, and he did not know anyone.\nOur class has two kinds of boys, and Yossi and Nosson are the best examples. Yossi talks to everyone. In the first week of school he knew every boy's name, his birthday and his favorite baseball team. Nosson hardly talks at all. He sits in the back, draws in the margins of his notebook, and answers only when the rebbe calls on him.\nAt recess, Yossi bounced right up to Ephraim. \"Do you play basketball? What's your team? Do you have brothers? Where do you live?\" Ephraim's eyes darted from Yossi to the door. He answered each question with one word and backed toward the wall.\nNosson watched from the bench. After a few minutes, he walked over quietly and held out his notebook. On the page was a drawing of the CN Tower, the tall tower in Toronto. \"I saw it in a book,\" Nosson said. \"Did you ever go up?\"\nEphraim's whole face changed. \"Twice,\" he said. \"There's a glass floor at the top. You can look straight down.\" For the rest of recess, the two of them sat on the bench, and Nosson drew while Ephraim described the city.\nOn the walk home, Yossi caught up with me. \"I think I talked too much,\" he admitted. Then he grinned. \"Tomorrow I'll ask just one question. Maybe two.\"","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":6,"words":256},"PF.R.L07.S2.A":{"id":"PF.R.L07.S2.A","title":"The Spelling Bee","text":"The gym was so quiet that Yehuda could hear the fluorescent lights buzz. Only two boys were left on the stage: Yehuda and Akiva, the boy who had beaten him in every spelling bee since second grade.\nYehuda had practiced for months. Every night after supper, his father read him words from the long blue list, and every night he wrote the ones he missed ten times each. His notebook had grown fat with crossed-out letters.\nAkiva went first. \"Rhythm,\" said the judge. Akiva closed his eyes, spelled it slowly, and got it right. The audience clapped politely.\n\"Necessary,\" the judge said to Yehuda. His stomach tightened. One c, two s's, or two c's and one s? He had written this word ten times just last week, but now the letters seemed to swim in front of his eyes. \"N-E-C-E-S-S-A-R-Y,\" he said. The bell rang. Correct.\nThey went back and forth for six rounds. Then the judge gave Akiva the word \"embarrass.\" Akiva hesitated, his face reddening, and spelled it with one r. The bell sounded flat and final.\nNow Yehuda needed only one more word. As he walked to the microphone, he glanced at Akiva, who was staring at his shoes. Yehuda remembered how that felt; he had stared at his own shoes for five years.\nHe spelled \"occasion\" correctly and won.\nThe audience cheered, but the first thing Yehuda did was cross the stage. He held out his hand. \"You made me practice harder than I ever have,\" he told Akiva. \"Without you, I'd never have learned half these words.\"\nAkiva looked up, surprised, and then he shook Yehuda's hand firmly. \"Next year,\" he said, smiling for the first time all morning.\n\"Next year,\" Yehuda agreed.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":7,"words":288},"PF.R.L07.S2.B":{"id":"PF.R.L07.S2.B","title":"The Kite","text":"Mordechai had spent three Sundays building the kite. He had cut the frame from thin wooden strips, stretched red paper across it, and tied on a tail of bright ribbons, one for each of his brothers. When he finished, it was the most beautiful thing he had ever made, and he hung it on his bedroom wall until the wind was right.\nOn the first windy afternoon in spring, he carried it to the park with his little brother Tzvi. The wind grabbed the kite almost at once and pulled it high above the trees. Mordechai let out the string, laughing, until the kite was a red dot against the clouds.\n\"Can I hold it?\" Tzvi begged. \"Just for one minute?\"\nMordechai hesitated. Tzvi was only six. But his brother's eyes were shining, so he handed him the string. \"Hold on tight,\" he said.\nFor a few seconds, Tzvi held on. Then a gust snatched the string from his small fingers. The kite lurched, spun, and drifted away over the rooftops, its ribbons waving like a goodbye.\nTzvi's face crumpled. He waited for Mordechai to shout.\nMordechai felt the anger rise up in his chest, hot and sharp. Three Sundays, gone. But when he looked down at his brother, he saw how small and frightened Tzvi looked, and the anger drained away.\n\"Hey,\" he said, kneeling down. \"It was a really good flight, wasn't it? Do you know what that means? It means I know how to build one. Next Sunday, you can help me make a new kite. A blue one.\"\nTzvi sniffed. \"Can I cut the ribbons?\"\n\"You can cut all the ribbons.\"\nOn the walk home, Tzvi held his hand the whole way.","listen":false,"kind":"story","status":"Draft","level":7,"words":286},"PF.R.L08.I2.A":{"id":"PF.R.L08.I2.A","title":"The First Railroad Across America","text":"Source 1: Joining a Nation\nIn the 1850s, a trip from New York to California could take months. Travelers chose between a long, dangerous wagon trail across the plains and mountains, or a sea voyage around South America. Many people believed the country needed a railroad that crossed the whole continent.\nIn 1862, Congress passed a law to build one. Two companies would race toward each other. The Central Pacific started in Sacramento, California, and built east. The Union Pacific started near Omaha, Nebraska, and built west. The government paid the companies in land and loans for every mile of track they laid, which pushed both to build as fast as possible.\nOn May 10, 1869, the two lines met at Promontory Summit, in Utah. A ceremonial golden spike was tapped into the last tie, and a message was sent by telegraph: \"Done.\" Suddenly, a trip across the country took about a week instead of months. Farmers could ship crops to distant cities, and new towns sprang up along the tracks.\nSource 2: The Hands That Built It\nThe railroad was built by tens of thousands of workers, and many of them were immigrants. The Central Pacific hired thousands of workers from China. They faced the hardest stretch of all: the granite of the Sierra Nevada mountains. Working with hand drills and black powder, they blasted tunnels through solid rock, sometimes advancing only a foot a day. Winters brought snow so deep that some crews lived and worked in tunnels under the drifts.\nOn the Union Pacific side, many workers had come from Ireland, and many others were soldiers who had just returned from the Civil War. They laid track across the open plains at a remarkable pace, sometimes several miles in a single day. Their dangers were different: summer heat, winter storms, and long stretches far from any town.\nFor decades, the workers from China received little credit. In the famous photograph of the 1869 ceremony, almost none of them appear. Today, historians and descendants work to make sure their part in the story is remembered.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":8,"words":347,"sources":2},"PF.R.L08.I2.B":{"id":"PF.R.L08.I2.B","title":"The Panama Canal","text":"Source 1: A Shortcut Between Oceans\nBefore 1914, a ship sailing from New York to San Francisco had to travel all the way around the southern tip of South America. The voyage was long, expensive and often stormy. For centuries, people dreamed of cutting a path across the narrow strip of land in Panama, where the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans are only about fifty miles apart.\nFrance tried first, in the 1880s, but gave up after years of trouble. In 1904, the United States took over the project. Engineers decided not to dig a canal at sea level. Instead, they built a dam to create a huge lake in the middle of the country and a series of locks, giant water-filled chambers that lift ships up to the lake and lower them back down on the other side.\nThe canal opened in 1914. A trip from New York to San Francisco became about 8,000 miles shorter. Shipping became faster and cheaper, and trade between the two coasts of the United States, and between many countries, grew quickly.\nSource 2: The War Against the Mosquito\nThe greatest danger in Panama was not the digging. It was disease. Yellow fever and malaria had killed thousands of workers during the French attempt. At the time, many people still blamed the illnesses on \"bad air\" from the swamps.\nAn army doctor named William Gorgas believed, based on new research, that mosquitoes spread both diseases. He organized a huge campaign: workers drained standing water, covered rain barrels, spread oil on ponds where mosquitoes laid eggs, and put screens on buildings. Many people laughed at his plan at first, and some officials refused to pay for it. Gorgas kept going. Within a few years, yellow fever disappeared from the Canal Zone, and malaria dropped sharply.\nMany historians believe the canal could not have been finished without this work. By protecting the health of the workers, Gorgas made it possible for the engineers to complete their plan.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":8,"words":331,"sources":2},"PF.R.L09.I3.A":{"id":"PF.R.L09.I3.A","title":"Should the Yeshiva Start Later?","text":"Source 1: Letter from the Grade 8 Student Council\nWe ask the yeshiva to move the start of the day from 7:45 to 8:30. Many of us go to sleep late because of homework and night seder, and we are exhausted in the morning. Last month we asked forty boys in grades 6 to 8, and thirty-one said they often feel too tired to concentrate during first period. Several boys told us they fall asleep during the first shiur, and two said they skip breakfast because there is no time. We also noticed that the parking lot is crowded and noisy at 7:40, when parents and buses all arrive at once. We think a later start would make boys happier and more alert, and that it would make mornings calmer for families too. Everyone we spoke to agreed that this change is a good idea.\nSource 2: Summary from a National Doctors' Group\nIn 2014, a national group of children's doctors recommended that middle schools and high schools start no earlier than 8:30 in the morning. The group explained that during the teenage years, the body's natural sleep schedule shifts later, so teenagers often cannot fall asleep early even when they try. Studies of schools that moved to later start times found that students slept more on school nights, and some schools reported fewer late arrivals. The group noted that a later start alone does not solve the problem; families also need regular bedtimes and less screen time at night.\nSource 3: Letter from the Bus Company\nWe were asked whether buses could arrive at 8:30 instead of 7:45. Our drivers also serve two other schools in the morning, and the routes are planned around the current times. A later start for the yeshiva would require either new drivers or later times for the other schools. New drivers would raise the yeshiva's cost by an estimated $18,000 per year. Another possibility would be to keep the current bus times and let boys arrive early, with a supervised room open from 7:45, but that would not help boys who need more sleep. We are willing to discuss options, but we cannot promise the change for this school year. If the yeshiva decides to change its schedule for next year, we would need to know by the end of February to plan new routes and hire drivers.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":9,"words":403,"sources":3},"PF.R.L09.I3.B":{"id":"PF.R.L09.I3.B","title":"Should Our Textbooks Be on Tablets?","text":"Source 1: Letter from the Grade 8 Student Council\nWe ask the yeshiva to replace our paper textbooks with tablets. Our backpacks are far too heavy: one boy in our grade weighed his, and it came to twenty-two pounds. Tablets would hold every book in one light device, and they could include videos and practice games. We believe students would learn much more with tablets. Several boys have complained of sore shoulders, and the school nurse told us that heavy backpacks can strain a growing back. Tablets would also save paper, and a teacher could update a book instantly instead of waiting for a new printing. Some of us already read books on tablets at home and find it easy and fun. Every boy in our grade wants this change.\nSource 2: Advertisement from a Tablet Company\nBring your school into the future with the StudyPad! Schools that switch to StudyPads see grades rise by as much as 30 percent. Our lightweight tablet holds thousands of books, and its bright screen makes reading a pleasure. Teachers love our easy online dashboard, and parents love knowing exactly what their sons are learning. Thousands of happy schools already trust StudyPad. Order now and receive free cases for every student. Don't let your students fall behind!\nSource 3: Summary from a University Review of Research\nResearchers reviewed dozens of studies that compared reading on paper with reading on screens. They found that for long texts, such as textbook chapters, students on average understood a little more when they read on paper. The difference was small, and it was smaller for short texts. The researchers suggested several possible reasons, including that students tend to skim more quickly on screens. They also found that screens were useful for some tasks, such as looking up information. They also noted that many of the studies were small and lasted only a few weeks, so more research is needed, especially on younger students. Some schools in the studies used a mix: paper books for long reading and screens for research and practice. They concluded that schools should consider which kinds of reading students will do before deciding to switch, and that teaching students to slow down when reading on screens may help.","listen":false,"kind":"info","status":"Draft","level":9,"words":374,"sources":3}},"items":[{"id":"PF.M.MATH.NUM.L01.01.A","skill":"MATH.NUM.L01.01","subject":"MATH","strand":"NPV","level":1,"ccss":"K.CC.B.4a","fmt":"CHOICE","stem":"Tap the row with one hat for each boy.","say":"Tap the row with one hat for each boy.","ans":"4 hats","wrong":[["3 hats","CNT-SKIP"],["5 hats","CNT-DOUBLE"],["3 hats spread out wide","SIZE-NOT-COUNT"]],"b":-4.33,"form":"A","status":"Approved","show":"","cmode":"text","screenNote":"4 boys in a row","audioOnly":false,"rw":11},{"id":"PF.M.MATH.NUM.L01.01.B","skill":"MATH.NUM.L01.01","subject":"MATH","strand":"NPV","level":1,"ccss":"K.CC.B.4a","fmt":"CHOICE","stem":"Tap the row with one cup for each boy.","say":"Tap the row with one cup for each boy.","ans":"6 cups","wrong":[["5 cups","CNT-SKIP"],["7 cups","CNT-DOUBLE"],["4 cups spread out wide","SIZE-NOT-COUNT"]],"b":-4.33,"form":"B","status":"Approved","show":"","cmode":"text","screenNote":"6 boys at a Shabbos table","audioOnly":false,"rw":11},{"id":"PF.M.MATH.NUM.L01.01.C","skill":"MATH.NUM.L01.01","subject":"MATH","strand":"NPV","level":1,"ccss":"K.CC.B.4a","fmt":"CHOICE","stem":"Tap the row with one siddur for each boy.","say":"Tap the row with one siddur for each boy.","ans":"3 siddurim","wrong":[["2 siddurim","CNT-SKIP"],["4 siddurim","CNT-DOUBLE"],["2 siddurim spread out wide","SIZE-NOT-COUNT"]],"b":-4.33,"form":"C","status":"Approved","show":"","cmode":"text","screenNote":"3 boys","audioOnly":false,"rw":11},{"id":"PF.M.MATH.NUM.L01.02.A","skill":"MATH.NUM.L01.02","subject":"MATH","strand":"NPV","level":1,"ccss":"K.CC.B.5","fmt":"TYPE","stem":"How many stars?","say":"How many stars?","ans":"7","wrong":[["6","CNT-SKIP"],["8","CNT-DOUBLE"]],"b":-4.33,"form":"A","status":"Approved","show":"","cmode":"text","screenNote":"7 stars, scattered","audioOnly":false,"rw":3},{"id":"PF.M.MATH.NUM.L01.02.B","skill":"MATH.NUM.L01.02","subject":"MATH","strand":"NPV","level":1,"ccss":"K.CC.B.5","fmt":"TYPE","stem":"How many apples?","say":"How many apples?","ans":"9","wrong":[["8","CNT-SKIP"],["10","CNT-DOUBLE"]],"b":-4.33,"form":"B","status":"Approved","show":"","cmode":"text","screenNote":"9 apples in a row","audioOnly":false,"rw":3},{"id":"PF.M.MATH.NUM.L01.02.C","skill":"MATH.NUM.L01.02","subject":"MATH","strand":"NPV","level":1,"ccss":"K.CC.B.5","fmt":"TYPE","stem":"How many dreidels?","say":"How many dreidels?","ans":"5","wrong":[["4","CNT-SKIP"],["6","CNT-DOUBLE"]],"b":-4.33,"form":"C","status":"Approved","show":"","cmode":"text","screenNote":"5 dreidels in a circle","audioOnly":false,"rw":3},{"id":"PF.M.MATH.NUM.L01.03.A","skill":"MATH.NUM.L01.03","subject":"MATH","strand":"NPV","level":1,"ccss":"K.CC.A.3","fmt":"TYPE","stem":"Type the number you hear.","say":"Type the number you hear. fourteen","ans":"14","wrong":[["41","REV"],["40","NAME-SOUND"]],"b":-4.28,"form":"A","status":"Approved","show":"","cmode":"text","screenNote":"","audioOnly":false,"rw":3},{"id":"PF.M.MATH.NUM.L01.03.B","skill":"MATH.NUM.L01.03","subject":"MATH","strand":"NPV","level":1,"ccss":"K.CC.A.3","fmt":"TYPE","stem":"Type the number you hear.","say":"Type the number you hear. seventeen","ans"